#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "Upcoming breaking changes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:6
msgid ""
"The Git project aims to ensure backwards compatibility to the best extent "
"possible. Minor releases will not break backwards compatibility unless there "
"is a very strong reason to do so, like for example a security vulnerability."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:11
msgid ""
"Regardless of that, due to the age of the Git project, it is only natural to "
"accumulate a backlog of backwards-incompatible changes that will eventually "
"be required to keep the project aligned with a changing world. These changes "
"fall into several categories:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:13
msgid "Changes to long established defaults."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:14
msgid "Concepts that have been replaced with a superior design."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:17
msgid ""
"Concepts, commands, configuration or options that have been lacking in major "
"ways and that cannot be fixed and which will thus be removed without any "
"replacement."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:20
msgid ""
"Explicitly not included in this list are fixes to minor bugs that may cause "
"a change in user-visible behavior."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:26
msgid ""
"The Git project irregularly releases breaking versions that deliberately "
"break backwards compatibility with older versions. This is done to ensure "
"that Git remains relevant, safe and maintainable going forward. The release "
"cadence of breaking versions is typically measured in multiple years. We had "
"the following major breaking releases in the past:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:28
msgid "Git 1.6.0, released in August 2008."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:29
msgid "Git 2.0, released in May 2014."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:36
msgid ""
"We use <major>.<minor> release numbers these days, starting from Git "
"2.0. For future releases, our plan is to increment <major> in the release "
"number when we make the next breaking release. Before Git 2.0, the release "
"numbers were 1.<major>.<minor> with the intention to increment <major> for "
"\"usual\" breaking releases, reserving the jump to Git 2.0 for really large "
"backward-compatibility breaking changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:41
msgid ""
"The intent of this document is to track upcoming deprecations for future "
"breaking releases. Furthermore, this document also tracks what will _not_ be "
"deprecated. This is done such that the outcome of discussions document both "
"when the discussion favors deprecation, but also when it rejects a "
"deprecation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:46
msgid ""
"Items should have a clear summary of the reasons why we do or do not want to "
"make the described change that can be easily understood without having to "
"read the mailing list discussions. If there are alternatives to the changed "
"feature, those alternatives should be pointed out to our users."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:50
msgid ""
"All items should be accompanied by references to relevant mailing list "
"threads where the deprecation was discussed. These references use "
"message-IDs, which can visited via"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:52
#, no-wrap
msgid "  https://lore.kernel.org/git/$message_id/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:56
msgid ""
"to see the message and its surrounding discussion. Such a reference is there "
"to make it easier for you to find how the project reached consensus on the "
"described item back then."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:61
msgid ""
"This is a living document as the environment surrounding the project changes "
"over time. If circumstances change, an earlier decision to deprecate or "
"change something may need to be revisited from time to time. So do not take "
"items on this list to mean \"it is settled, do not waste our time bringing "
"it up again\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:62
#, no-wrap
msgid "Procedure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:71
msgid ""
"Discussing the desire to make breaking changes, declaring that breaking "
"changes are made at a certain version boundary, and recording these "
"decisions in this document, are necessary but not sufficient.  Because such "
"changes are expected to be numerous, and the design and implementation of "
"them are expected to span over time, they have to be deployable trivially at "
"such a version boundary, prepared over long time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:78
msgid ""
"The breaking changes MUST be guarded with the a compile-time switch, "
"WITH_BREAKING_CHANGES, to help this process.  When built with it, the "
"resulting Git binary together with its documentation would behave as if "
"these breaking changes slated for the next big version boundary are already "
"in effect.  We also have a CI job to exercise the work-in-progress version "
"of Git with these breaking changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "Git 3.0"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:84
msgid ""
"The following subsections document upcoming breaking changes for Git "
"3.0. There is no planned release date for this breaking version yet."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:88
msgid ""
"Proposed changes and removals only include items which are \"ready\" to be "
"done.  In other words, this is not supposed to be a wishlist of features "
"that should be changed to or replaced in case the alternative was "
"implemented already."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:89
#, no-wrap
msgid "Changes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:95
msgid ""
"The default hash function for new repositories will be changed from \"sha1\" "
"to \"sha256\". SHA-1 has been deprecated by NIST in 2011 and is nowadays "
"recommended against in FIPS 140-2 and similar certifications. Furthermore, "
"there are practical attacks on SHA-1 that weaken its cryptographic "
"properties:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:98
msgid ""
"The SHAppening (2015). The first demonstration of a practical attack against "
"SHA-1 with 2^57 operations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:99
msgid "SHAttered (2017). Generation of two valid PDF files with 2^63 operations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:101
msgid ""
"Birthday-Near-Collision (2019). This attack allows for chosen prefix attacks "
"with 2^68 operations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:103
msgid ""
"Shambles (2020). This attack allows for chosen prefix attacks with 2^63 "
"operations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:110
msgid ""
"While we have protections in place against known attacks, it is expected "
"that more attacks against SHA-1 will be found by future research. Paired "
"with the ever-growing capability of hardware, it is only a matter of time "
"before SHA-1 will be considered broken completely. We want to be prepared "
"and will thus change the default hash algorithm to \"sha256\" for newly "
"initialized repositories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:114
msgid ""
"An important requirement for this change is that the ecosystem is ready to "
"support the \"sha256\" object format. This includes popular Git libraries, "
"applications and forges."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:116
msgid ""
"There is no plan to deprecate the \"sha1\" object format at this point in "
"time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:120
msgid ""
"Cf. <2f5de416-04ba-c23d-1e0b-83bb655829a7@zombino.com>, "
"<20170223155046.e7nxivfwqqoprsqj@LykOS.localdomain>, "
"<CA+EOSBncr=4a4d8n9xS4FNehyebpmX8JiUwCsXD47EQDE+DiUQ@mail.gmail.com>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:124
msgid ""
"The default storage format for references in newly created repositories will "
"be changed from \"files\" to \"reftable\". The \"reftable\" format provides "
"multiple advantages over the \"files\" format:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:129
msgid ""
"It is impossible to store two references that only differ in casing on "
"case-insensitive filesystems with the \"files\" format. This issue is common "
"on Windows and macOS platforms. As the \"reftable\" backend does not use "
"filesystem paths to encode reference names this problem goes away."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:133
msgid ""
"Similarly, macOS normalizes path names that contain unicode characters, "
"which has the consequence that you cannot store two names with unicode "
"characters that are encoded differently with the \"files\" backend. Again, "
"this is not an issue with the \"reftable\" backend."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:138
msgid ""
"Deleting references with the \"files\" backend requires Git to rewrite the "
"complete \"packed-refs\" file. In large repositories with many references "
"this file can easily be dozens of megabytes in size, in extreme cases it may "
"be gigabytes. The \"reftable\" backend uses tombstone markers for deleted "
"references and thus does not have to rewrite all of its data."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:146
msgid ""
"Repository housekeeping with the \"files\" backend typically performs "
"all-into-one repacks of references. This can be quite expensive, and "
"consequently housekeeping is a tradeoff between the number of loose "
"references that accumulate and slow down operations that read references, "
"and compressing those loose references into the \"packed-refs\" file. The "
"\"reftable\" backend uses geometric compaction after every write, which "
"amortizes costs and ensures that the backend is always in a well-maintained "
"state."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:150
msgid ""
"Operations that write multiple references at once are not atomic with the "
"\"files\" backend. Consequently, Git may see in-between states when it reads "
"references while a reference transaction is in the process of being "
"committed to disk."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:154
msgid ""
"Writing many references at once is slow with the \"files\" backend because "
"every reference is created as a separate file. The \"reftable\" backend "
"significantly outperforms the \"files\" backend by multiple orders of "
"magnitude."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:157
msgid ""
"The reftable backend uses a binary format with prefix compression for "
"reference names. As a result, the format uses less space compared to the "
"\"packed-refs\" file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:163
msgid ""
"Users that get immediate benefit from the \"reftable\" backend could "
"continue to opt-in to the \"reftable\" format manually by setting the "
"\"init.defaultRefFormat\" config. But defaults matter, and we think that "
"overall users will have a better experience with less platform-specific "
"quirks when they use the new backend by default."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:167
msgid ""
"A prerequisite for this change is that the ecosystem is ready to support the "
"\"reftable\" format. Most importantly, alternative implementations of Git "
"like JGit, libgit2 and Gitoxide need to support it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:173
msgid ""
"In new repositories, the default branch name will be `main`. We have been "
"warning that the default name will change since 675704c74dd (init: provide "
"useful advice about init.defaultBranch, 2020-12-11).  The new name matches "
"the default branch name used in new repositories by many of the big Git "
"forges."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:177
msgid ""
"Git will require Rust as a mandatory part of the build process. While Git "
"already started to adopt Rust in Git 2.49, all parts written in Rust are "
"optional for the time being. This includes:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:180
msgid ""
"The Rust wrapper around libgit.a that is part of \"contrib/\" and which has "
"been introduced in Git 2.49."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:182
msgid ""
"Subsystems that have an alternative implementation in Rust to test "
"interoperability between our C and Rust codebase."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:184
msgid ""
"Newly written features that are not mission critical for a fully functional "
"Git client."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:188
msgid ""
"These changes are meant as test balloons to allow distributors of Git to "
"prepare for Rust becoming a mandatory part of the build process. There will "
"be multiple milestones for the introduction of Rust:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:193
msgid ""
"Initially, with Git 2.52, support for Rust will be auto-detected by Meson "
"and disabled in our Makefile so that the project can sort out the initial "
"infrastructure."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:197
msgid ""
"In Git 2.53, both build systems will default-enable support for Rust.  "
"Consequently, builds will break by default if Rust is not available on the "
"build host. The use of Rust can still be explicitly disabled via build "
"flags."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:199
msgid ""
"In Git 3.0, the build options will be removed and support for Rust is "
"mandatory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:204
msgid ""
"You can explicitly ask both Meson and our Makefile-based system to enable "
"Rust by saying `meson configure -Drust=enabled` and `make "
"WITH_RUST=YesPlease`, respectively."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:212
msgid ""
"The Git project will declare the last version before Git 3.0 to be a "
"long-term support release. This long-term release will receive important bug "
"fixes for at least four release cycles and security fixes for six release "
"cycles. The Git project will hand over maintainership of the long-term "
"release to distributors in case they need to extend the life of that "
"long-term release even further.  Details of how this long-term release will "
"be handed over to the community will be discussed once the Git project "
"decides to stop officially supporting it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:218
msgid ""
"We will evaluate the impact on downstream distributions before making Rust "
"mandatory in Git 3.0. If we see that the impact on downstream distributions "
"would be significant, we may decide to defer this change to a subsequent "
"minor release. This evaluation will also take into account our own "
"experience with how painful it is to keep Rust an optional component."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:219
#, no-wrap
msgid "Removals"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:223
msgid ""
"Support for grafting commits has long been superseded by git-replace(1).  "
"Grafts are inferior to replacement refs:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:226
msgid "Grafts are a local-only mechanism and cannot be shared across repositories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:228
msgid ""
"Grafts can lead to hard-to-diagnose problems when transferring objects "
"between repositories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:231
msgid ""
"The grafting mechanism has been marked as outdated since e650d0643b (docs: "
"mark info/grafts as outdated, 2014-03-05) and will be removed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:233
msgid "Cf. <20140304174806.GA11561@sigill.intra.peff.net>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:240
msgid ""
"The git-pack-redundant(1) command can be used to remove redundant pack "
"files.  The subcommand is unusably slow and the reason why nobody reports it "
"as a performance bug is suspected to be the absence of users. We have "
"nominated the command for removal and have started to emit a user-visible "
"warning in c3b58472be (pack-redundant: gauge the usage before proposing its "
"removal, 2020-08-25) whenever the command is executed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:246
msgid ""
"So far there was a single complaint about somebody still using the command, "
"but that complaint did not cause us to reverse course. On the contrary, we "
"have doubled down on the deprecation and starting with 4406522b76 "
"(pack-redundant: escalate deprecation warning to an error, 2023-03-23), the "
"command dies unless the user passes the `--i-still-use-this` option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:249
msgid ""
"There have not been any subsequent complaints, so this command will finally "
"be removed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:253
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Cf. <xmqq1rjuz6n3.fsf_-_@gitster.c.googlers.com>,\n"
"    <CAKvOHKAFXQwt4D8yUCCkf_TQL79mYaJ=KAKhtpDNTvHJFuX1NA@mail.gmail.com>,\n"
"    <20230323204047.GA9290@coredump.intra.peff.net>,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:257
msgid ""
"Support for storing shorthands for remote URLs in "
"\"$GIT_COMMON_DIR/branches/\" and \"$GIT_COMMON_DIR/remotes/\" has been long "
"superseded by storing remotes in the repository configuration."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:268
msgid ""
"The mechanism has originally been introduced in f170e4b39d ([PATCH] "
"fetch/pull: short-hand notation for remote repositories., 2005-07-16) and "
"was superseded by 6687f8fea2 ([PATCH] Use .git/remote/origin, not "
".git/branches/origin., 2005-08-20), where we switched from "
"\".git/branches/\" to \".git/remotes/\". That commit already mentions an "
"upcoming deprecation of the \".git/branches/\" directory, and starting with "
"a1d4aa7424 (Add repository-layout document., 2005-09-01) we have also marked "
"this layout as deprecated. Eventually we also started to migrate away from "
"\".git/remotes/\" in favor of config-based remotes, and we have marked the "
"directory as legacy in 3d3d282146 (Documentation: Grammar correction, "
"wording fixes and cleanup, 2011-08-23)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:276
msgid ""
"As our documentation mentions, these directories are unlikely to be used in "
"modern repositories and most users aren't even aware of these "
"mechanisms. They have been deprecated for almost 20 years and 14 years "
"respectively, and we are not aware of any active users that have complained "
"about this deprecation.  Furthermore, the \".git/branches/\" directory is "
"nowadays misleadingly named and may cause confusion as \"branches\" are "
"almost exclusively used in the context of references."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:278
msgid "These features will be removed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:283
msgid ""
"Support for \"--stdin\" option in the \"name-rev\" command was deprecated "
"(and hidden from the documentation) in the Git 2.40 timeframe, in preference "
"to its synonym \"--annotate-stdin\".  Git 3.0 removes the support for "
"\"--stdin\" altogether."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:290
msgid ""
"The git-whatchanged(1) command has outlived its usefulness more than 10 "
"years ago, and takes more keystrokes to type than its rough equivalent `git "
"log --raw`.  We have nominated the command for removal, have changed the "
"command to refuse to work unless the `--i-still-use-this` option is given, "
"and asked the users to report when they do so."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:292
msgid "The command will be removed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:295
msgid ""
"Support for `core.commentString=auto` has been deprecated and will be "
"removed in Git 3.0."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:297
msgid "cf. <xmqqa59i45wc.fsf@gitster.g>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:302
msgid ""
"Support for `core.preferSymlinkRefs=true` has been deprecated and will be "
"removed in Git 3.0. Writing symbolic refs as symbolic links will be phased "
"out in favor of using plain files using the textual representation of "
"symbolic refs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:310
msgid ""
"Symbolic references were initially always stored as a symbolic link. This "
"was changed in 9b143c6e15 (Teach update-ref about a symbolic ref stored in a "
"textfile., 2005-09-25), where a new textual symref format was introduced to "
"store those symbolic refs in a plain file. In 9f0bb90d16 "
"(core.prefersymlinkrefs: use symlinks for .git/HEAD, 2006-05-02), the Git "
"project switched the default to use the textual symrefs in favor of symbolic "
"links."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:314
msgid ""
"The migration away from symbolic links has happened almost 20 years ago by "
"now, and there is no known reason why one should prefer them "
"nowadays. Furthermore, symbolic links are not supported on some platforms."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:317
msgid ""
"Note that only the writing side for such symbolic links is "
"deprecated. Reading such symbolic links is still supported for now."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:318
#, no-wrap
msgid "Superseded features that will not be deprecated"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:324
msgid ""
"Some features have gained newer replacements that aim to improve the design "
"in certain ways. The fact that there is a replacement does not automatically "
"mean that the old way of doing things will eventually be removed. This "
"section tracks those features with newer alternatives."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:329
msgid ""
"The features git-checkout(1) offers are covered by the pair of commands "
"git-restore(1) and git-switch(1). Because the use of git-checkout(1) is "
"still widespread, and it is not expected that this will change anytime soon, "
"all three commands will stay."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:332
msgid ""
"This decision may get revisited in case we ever figure out that there are "
"almost no users of any of the commands anymore."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/BreakingChanges.adoc:335
msgid ""
"Cf. <xmqqttjazwwa.fsf@gitster.g>, <xmqqleeubork.fsf@gitster.g>, "
"<112b6568912a6de6672bf5592c3a718e@manjaro.org>."
msgstr ""
